f stock prices and the role of computer-aided program trading . Taken together , the worries <ei26 prompted> a broad <ei27 sell-off> of stocks . The number of stocks on the New York Stock Exchange that <ei28 fell> in price yesterday exceeded 1,000 , a key measure of underlying sentiment among technical analysts . Although the government <ei30 said> the economy <ei31 grew> an estimated 2.5 % in the third quarter , in line with expectations , analysts are increasingly <ei33 predicting> much more sluggish <ei34 growth> -- and therefore more corporate earnings <ei36 disappointments> -- for the fourth quarter . \" There are a lot more downward <ei38 revisions> of earnings forecasts than upward <ei39 revisions> , \" <ei40 said> Abby Joseph Cohen , a market strategist at Drexel Burnham Lambert . \" People are <ei41 questioning> corporate profits as a pillar of support for the equity market . \" The bond market was <ei42 unmoved> by the economic statistics . While bond investors would have <ei43 preferred> <ei44 growth> to <ei45 be> a little slower , they were <ei46 cheered> by inflation measures in the data that showed prices <ei47 rising> at a modest annual rate of 2.9 % . That is another small <ei48 encouragement> for the Federal Reserve to <ei49 lower> interest rates in coming weeks , they <ei51 reasoned> . In major market <ei52 activity> : Stock prices <ei53 fell> sharply in active trading . Volume on the New York Stock Exchange <ei54 totaled> 175.2 million shares . Declining issues on the Big Board <ei55 outstripped> gainers 1,141 to 406 . Bond prices were barely <ei56 higher> . The Treasury 's benchmark 30-year <ei58 rose> fractionally . Yield on the issue was 7.88 % . The dollar <ei59 rose> modestly against most major currencies . In late New York trading the dollar was at 1.8400 marks and 142.10 yen compared with 1.8353 marks and 141.52 yen Wednesday . The dollar <ei61 soared> against the pound , which was at $ 1.5765 compared with $ 1.6145 Wednesday .", "gold": [[